MetaWrap2 v=2.0.1
	Phase 1 foundation work: a pipeline driver, verified resume, a durable run history, one
		naming convention for bins, and a one-command install. No scientific behaviour
		changed - that is the point of this release.
	New - one command for a whole study
		"metawrap2 run <sheet|directory> -o study/" runs the pipeline end to end from a small
			TOML (or YAML) sample sheet. It owns the intermediate layout and the plumbing
			between steps that the tutorial previously asked users to do by hand with
			mkdir/mv/cat - collecting each sample's QC'ed reads into CLEAN_READS,
			concatenating them for a co-assembly, handing each module the right bin
			directory - and none of that was resumable or recorded before
		-j runs independent samples concurrently, dividing the -t thread budget between them
		--resume skips steps that already finished (state in <output>/.metawrap2/run_steps),
			--dry-run prints every module command it would run, --steps/--skip adjust the plan,
			--no-coassembly assembles per sample, and a directory can be passed instead of a
			sheet to pair up the reads in it automatically
		every step is an ordinary "metawrap2 <module>" invocation logged to study/LOGS/, so
			the driver adds no behaviour of its own and anything it does can be done by hand
		"metawrap2 run --example" prints a documented starter sheet
	New - read layouts and file naming
		paired files are now recognised in any of the usual conventions - _1/_2, _R1/_R2,
			_R1_001/_R2_001, .1/.2 - instead of only _1.fastq/_2.fastq. Illumina output no
			longer has to be renamed before MetaWrap2 will touch it. .gz and .bz2 throughout
		read_qc supports --single-end and --interleaved. Its error message had told users to
			pass those flags since 2.0.0, but neither existed. Interleaved input is split into
			mates first and then follows the ordinary paired path, because Trim Galore has no
			interleaved mode and treating it as single-end would destroy the pairing
		io/reads.py is now the single place that decides what a read filename means, and is
			actually wired in - it shipped in 2.0.0 fully tested but imported by nothing
	Fixed
		"metawrap2 run ... --dry-run" executed the run instead of planning it: the top-level
			CLI strips the global flags out of argv before a subcommand parses them, so the
			driver never saw its own --dry-run
		the driver passed a fixed -A/-B/-C to bin_refinement, so a study that selected only
			some binners failed with "INITIAL_BINNING/maxbin2_bins is not a valid directory";
			it now passes only the bin sets binning was asked to produce
		quant_bins deleted salmon's per-sample alignment directory only when there was more
			than one sample, so single-sample runs silently kept it
		bin_refinement rewrote every bin file on every invocation while normalising contig
			headers; it now only rewrites the files that actually change
		add_bins_to_blobplot keyed contigs on the whole FASTA header, so bins carrying
			metaBAT2's header annotations matched nothing and the blobplot came out entirely
			unbinned. Same contig-identity fix as the rest of 2.0.1
		a duplicated test function name meant one test had been silently shadowed and never run
	Changed - readability and lint
		choose_best_bin.py, filter_reads_for_bin_reassembly.py and
			filter_nanopore_reads_for_bin_reassembly.py were rewritten out of their
			tab-indented, module-level-sys.argv Python-2 form into documented, importable,
			tested functions. Behaviour is unchanged: the read-recruitment rewrite was checked
			byte-for-byte against the original over a real bwa-mem stream (all 12 output files
			identical), and choose_best_bin picks the same winners. Both now test SAM flags
			with bitwise masks rather than indexing into bin(flag), which raised IndexError on
			small flag values (the nanopore version had a try/except IndexError for exactly this)
		ruff now reports no findings anywhere in the repository, down from 564, and CI gates
			on it instead of running it with "|| true". Style-only rules that contradict the
			project's own conventions (printf formatting, typing.List for the py3.8 target) are
			ignored with a comment saying why; vendored code is excluded; genuinely unclosed
			file handles were fixed and deliberately long-lived ones annotated
		CI also smoke-tests the CLI: every module must print its help and plan a run with no
			external tool installed
		metawrap2.toml.example is generated from the same template "config init" writes, with a
			test that they have not drifted. The example had fallen behind and listed neither
			BLASTDB_NAME, CHECKM_DB, BAKTA_DB nor GTDBTK_DATA_PATH
	New - one command to install it all
		"metawrap2 quickstart" creates every module's conda environment, then verifies the
			result. Getting started previously meant four separate commands and a reading of
			the docs before anything ran; each was simple and none of them was what the user
			came to do. -t builds several environments at once, --binning-only installs just
			the six modules genome recovery needs rather than all thirteen, and --from-lock
			builds from the committed lockfiles
		it never downloads a database that was not asked for. The databases run from 550 MB
			to ~300 GB and take hours to days, and where they go is a decision about the
			machine, so --databases small|full|none is required to fetch anything. With no
			answer given it installs the environments, prints what each module needs and how
			large it is, and - only when a terminal is attached - offers to do it, defaulting
			to no. A non-interactive run never asks and never downloads
		it composes rather than reimplements: it calls the same install-env, install-db and
			doctor code paths, and stops at the first failing step naming the exact command to
			resume from
		"metawrap2 config migrate <file>" converts an older shell-style configuration - a file
			of KEY=/path lines meant to be sourced - into the TOML that is read now. It keeps
			the database paths, drops keys that no longer apply and says why, flags paths that
			do not exist on this machine, and leaves out the variables that only located the
			old installation's own scripts. The file is scanned, never executed
		retired module and flag names now explain themselves. "metawrap2 kraken" and
			--metabat1 used to produce an argument error that said nothing about why they were
			gone; each now names what to use instead
	Changed - a guess no longer stops a run
		the pre-flight disk-space estimate warns instead of refusing. It is a chosen multiple
			of the input size rather than a measurement, and it cannot know how large scratch
			gets at its peak because scratch is deleted when a step finishes - so it can be
			wrong in both directions, and the user knows things about their filesystem that it
			does not. strict_space_check = true under [settings] restores the hard stop, which
			is what you want on shared infrastructure where one run filling the disk is
			everyone's problem. --skip-space-check still turns it off entirely
		a config's unknown or retired *database* keys are now notes rather than errors.
			[databases] is deliberately open, so a locally patched module may read a key
			nothing here knows; and a leftover key from an older setup is harmless. Refusing to
			run binning because the config still mentioned a database only a removed module
			ever read was absurd. An unknown [settings] key is still an error - that namespace
			is small and entirely ours, and a typo there silently does nothing
	Changed - things that change your output or your scripts
		bins are now named bin_001.fasta, for every binner, counted from 1 and zero-padded.
			metaWRAP named them three ways at once: metaBAT2's came out bin.1.fa, MaxBin2's
			were renumbered bin.0.fa (from 0 - an arbitrary choice of metaWRAP's own), and
			CONCOCT's carried CONCOCT's cluster ids, so the same number meant a different
			thing in each folder. Unpadded numbers also sort bin.1, bin.10, bin.2, which
			mattered because bin sets are iterated in sorted filename order and refined bins
			are numbered in that order - so with ten or more bins the numbering depended on a
			lexicographic accident. Bin CONTENTS are unchanged; the golden tests assert the
			same contigs in the same bins. Scripts globbing *.fa need *.fasta, and if you hand
			bins to CheckM or GTDB-Tk yourself the flag is now -x fasta
		.stats files are now .stats.tsv and .contigs files .contigs.tsv, so an extension says
			what a file is. Contents unchanged; scripts globbing *.stats need *.stats.tsv
		Python 3.10 is the minimum (was 3.8). 3.8 and 3.9 are both past end of life and the
			type checker cannot target them; CI runs 3.10-3.13
		--resume now re-runs anything it cannot verify. A step previously skipped on the
			strength of a marker file alone will re-run if its outputs are missing or empty or
			its inputs have changed. A resume that used to "work" may now do real work,
			because it may have been skipping a step that never produced anything
	New - --resume that verifies instead of assuming
		a run manifest (manifest.json) records each step's command, duration, outcome, and
			every declared input and output with a content fingerprint. A step is skipped only
			if it completed, its outputs are all still present and non-empty, and none of its
			inputs has changed. Marker-file resume was wrong in two directions: a step whose
			outputs were deleted was skipped anyway, and a step whose inputs had changed was
			also skipped, silently mixing results from two different inputs
		fingerprints hash the size plus the first and last megabyte, so the check costs the
			same on a 100 GB library as on a small one. --strict-fingerprints hashes whole
			files, for the case where an edit between the sampled ends matters
		"metawrap2 status <study>" reads the manifest back: per-step status, duration, and what
			--resume would do next and why - taken from the same check --resume itself makes,
			not a reimplementation of it. --step NAME shows one step's inputs, outputs, command
			and measured cost; --audit verifies every recorded output is still present and
			non-empty; --json for scripting
		at the end of every run the same audit runs automatically. Recording an output is a
			claim and something now checks it: a step that reported success but produced
			nothing, one that declared no outputs at all (--resume can never verify it, so it
			degrades to the marker-file behaviour manifests replaced), or a file no step
			claims, are all reported then rather than discovered on the next resume
	New - failing at the cause, not three modules later
		inputs and intermediates are checked for zero bytes, wrong format, truncation
			mid-record, and truncated gzip streams before a tool is handed them - and every
			problem is reported at once rather than one per re-run. An empty FASTQ used to
			produce an empty assembly, then zero bins, then a CheckM error about file
			extensions, hours and three modules from the cause
		preflight disk-space estimates refuse to start a step the filesystem plausibly cannot
			hold (metaSPAdes needs roughly 12x its input in scratch). The global
			--skip-space-check flag, or skip_space_check = true, overrides
		an unknown [settings] key or top-level table in the config is now an error with a
			did-you-mean, rather than being silently ignored - "use_conda_env" used to look
			like it worked and do nothing. [databases] stays open to unrecognised keys, since a
			locally patched module may use its own, but a name that is nearly a known one is
			reported: CHECKM_DBB means CheckM silently gets no database
	New - surviving what actually goes wrong
		SIGTERM and SIGHUP are handled like Ctrl-C. A scheduler killing the job on a wall-clock
			or memory limit, or a dropped SSH session, used to kill the process outright,
			leaving the step recorded as "running" forever with nothing to explain it. Those
			signals now raise a KeyboardInterrupt subclass, so every finally already written
			runs, the outcome is recorded, and the exit code is 128 + the signal number
		one writer per output directory: a lock file created with O_EXCL means a second run
			writing the same -o fails immediately, naming who holds it, instead of interleaving
			with the first and leaving a plausible-looking directory whose contents came from
			two runs with different parameters. A lock whose process is gone on this host is
			taken over silently; one from another host cannot be checked, so it is believed,
			and --force overrides
		Ctrl-C marks the running step interrupted, so --resume redoes it rather than trusting
			half-written outputs
		the memory budget is divided across parallel workers, so "-m 64 -j 4" gives each worker
			16 GB rather than four workers each believing they own 64
		one scratch policy: [settings] scratch_dir, or $METAWRAP2_SCRATCH, decides temporary
			space for every module instead of each tool's own default scattering files. Unset,
			scratch stays inside the run's output directory - the one location the user chose
		the run log rotates at 256 MB, keeping 3 backups. It holds every line of every tool,
			and one pathological tool could otherwise fill the filesystem the run needs
	New - knowing and reproducing what you ran
		"metawrap2 history" is an append-only record of every run ever started on this machine,
			at ~/.metawrap2/history.jsonl. It lives outside any output directory, survives
			deleting one, and is never pruned. --module/--output/--since/--failed/--commands/
			--json/--stats filter and reshape it, and --diff RUN RUN compares two runs field by
			field: version, conda env, databases, command line. A history write can never fail
			a run
		conda lockfiles are committed for every shipped environment. "install-env --lock" writes
			envs/locks/<module>.<platform>.lock from "conda list --explicit --md5";
			--from-lock, and "doctor --fix --from-lock", restore from it. Plain
			"conda create --file" works too, so no extra tooling is needed
		run_environment.json records the environment's full conda package list, written before
			any tool runs - so a run that dies in its first command still says what it was
			running with
		the MetaWrap2 git commit is recorded alongside the version string, with the branch,
			git describe, and whether the working tree was dirty. Between releases "2.0.1"
			names many different trees, including one with local edits - the normal state of a
			package whose design invites editing the module files
		databases are identified, not just located: which of each database's defining files are
			present, their total size, and a content fingerprint from name/size/mtime. A
			database updated in place is now visible as a change rather than an
			identical-looking path, so "which analyses used the nt I just replaced?" has an
			answer
		each step's real cost is measured - peak RSS, CPU time, wall time - by reaping the child
			with os.wait4 rather than subprocess.call. getrusage(RUSAGE_CHILDREN) cannot do
			this: it reports a running maximum across every child ever waited for. "1.0 cores
			busy while given 24 threads" is visible in the log
		"metawrap2 calibrate <study>..." compares the built-in disk and memory estimates against
			real runs. Output-to-input ratio is a measurable lower bound on the disk multiplier
			(scratch is deleted when a step finishes, so no manifest can record its peak);
			peak memory is measured exactly. The report says which is which, because a
			confidently wrong disk estimate is how a run fills a filesystem four hours in
	New - bin_refinement takes any number of bin sets
		up to six bin sets, not exactly two or three, with --refine-combinations
			pairs-and-all|pairs|all choosing which subsets are refined. Two- and three-set runs
			produce byte-identical output and an identical Binning_refiner command line to
			before, which the golden tests enforce
	New - metawrap2 doctor
		"metawrap2 doctor" is the same command as "metawrap2 test" under the name people reach
			for when something is broken - and the name its own repair flag already used.
			--fix recreates exactly the environments that are missing or broken, leaves healthy
			ones alone, then re-checks and reports anything still wrong
		doctor also checks the databases each module needs, using the same definitions as the
			provenance fingerprints. A healthy environment with a half-downloaded database is
			not a working installation, and checking envs alone answered only half of "can I
			run this?"
	Fixed - wrong or missing records
		every module recorded "completed" in its history regardless of outcome. finish_run is
			called from a finally, where success and failure are indistinguishable from the
			arguments, and it defaulted to the happy path - so a run that failed, or was
			killed, or called error(), still logged success and told you nothing. The status is
			now derived from whether an exception is propagating and which one
		metaBAT2's bins were never renumbered, so its output kept the tool's own bin.1.fa naming
			while MaxBin2's and CONCOCT's were rewritten. Renumbering now happens after
			metaBAT2's non-genome outputs (bin.unbinned.fa, bin.lowDepth.fa, bin.tooShort.fa)
			are moved aside, so a rejected-contigs file can never consume a bin number, and
			bins are ordered by the number metaBAT2 gave them numerically rather than
			lexicographically
		"metawrap2 doctor" was unreachable under that name despite its own repair flag being
			--fix; only "metawrap2 test" resolved
		a dead branch in check_fasta's truncation test: "A or B and C" parses as "A or (B and
			C)", making the single-line case unreachable. The remaining condition covered it
		bin_refinement left bins* directories in the output root for more than three input sets,
			and the binning/reassembly plots crashed or reused colours with many sets
		dereplication crashed with KeyError on a bin with no row in the stats file - which
			happens when a run is interrupted between writing bins and re-scoring them. Such a
			bin is now treated as the worst possible bin, with a warning, rather than aborting
			the refinement
		--subsample in kraken2 needed many times the file size in RAM, making it unusable on
			exactly the large libraries it exists to shrink. Reservoir sampling now bounds peak
			memory by the requested depth
	Changed - logging and typing
		logging is a real logging.Logger throughout, with a banner formatter on the console and
			a plain one in the file. Every run writes <output>/metawrap2.log; wrapped tools'
			stdout and stderr are funnelled into it at DEBUG, tagged [stdout]/[stderr], while
			still streaming to the screen. The console handler resolves sys.stdout at emit
			time, which fixes silent or erroring log output when the stream is swapped
		repetitive tool output is collapsed and progress-bar spam filtered from the captured
			logs - measured at 1.8 MB down to 30 KB on a real three-sample run.
			--verbose-logs restores the raw stream
		tqdm progress bars wherever MetaWrap2 itself is doing the work - splitting bins,
			subsampling reads, scanning FASTA - not only where a wrapped tool prints something
		type annotations throughout, checked by mypy in CI and in the pre-push hook
		the package is pip-installable as an ordinary wheel. The environment yamls and
			lockfiles moved under src/metawrap2/envs/ and ship as package data, so they
			resolve from an installed wheel and not only from a source checkout
		the README now describes MetaWrap2 rather than mirroring the metaWRAP 1 README
	Changed - the test suite gates a push
		456 tests, up from 245. New suites cover the manifest and its audit, input validation,
			the run history, the scratch policy, the lockfiles, "doctor --fix", the run lock,
			signal handling, "status", and bin naming
		15 golden tests pin bin_refinement's exact output - real Binning_refiner, real
			consolidation and dereplication, with only CheckM replaced by a deterministic
			stand-in - so the N-set generalisation could be proved not to change the 2- and
			3-set results
		a golden end-to-end test runs binning into bin_refinement, quant_bins and annotate_bins
			against stub tools on PATH, pinning the output tree and the .stats.tsv contract
			byte-for-byte. It catches what unit tests structurally cannot: a module writing
			where the next one does not look
		black, isort, ruff, mypy and pytest must all pass before a push (pre-commit pre-push
			hooks) and all run in CI on Python 3.10-3.13. Formatting is checked, never
			applied, in CI: a job that rewrites your code and then passes teaches you nothing
		ruff-format was removed from pre-commit - two formatters fight each other
		a weekly Lockfiles workflow creates conda environments from the committed lockfiles -
			the only check that they do what they claim - and opens a PR when a re-solve makes
			them drift, so drift arrives as a reviewable diff rather than a surprise months
			later. It also runs on any PR touching src/metawrap2/envs/

MetaWrap2 v=2.0.1
	Bug fixes and install/usability work from a full end-to-end validation run on real data
	(3 metaHIT gut metagenomes, every module, all databases).
	Fixed - would break any real run
		MetaWrap2's own helper scripts (metawrap2.scripts.*, metawrap2.vendor.*) were invoked
			as "python -m ..." *inside* a module's conda env, which contains only that
			module's external tools - no python, no metawrap2, no matplotlib. Every such call
			failed. They now run in the host interpreter by absolute path (metawrap2.pyrun);
			affected bin_refinement, reassemble_bins, quant_bins, and blobology
		"mamba run --no-capture-output" is a conda-only flag; mamba 2.x mis-parses it and
			every wrapped command died with "exec: --: invalid option", which looked like the
			tool was missing. The flag is now used only when "run --help" advertises it
		"metawrap2 test" probed tools with "bash -lc", and a login shell rebuilds PATH from
			the user's profile, discarding the env's own bin directory - so every tool in
			every healthy env was reported MISSING. Now uses "bash -c"
		numpy/pandas/matplotlib/seaborn were undeclared dependencies even though the bundled
			plotting helpers import them; added (plus tqdm for progress bars)
		envs/blobology.yaml pip-installed "metawrap2" from PyPI, where it does not exist, so
			the env could never be created. The blobology env now holds only external tools
		envs/classify_bins.yaml was missing blast, though the module shells out to blastn
		envs/assembly.yaml did not pin python, so QUAST landed on python>=3.12 and died with
			"No module named 'distutils'"; pinned python<3.12
		envs/bin_refinement.yaml did not pin setuptools, so CheckM1 died with "No module
			named 'pkg_resources'" on setuptools>=81; pinned setuptools<81
	Fixed - wrong results from contig-identity mismatches
		contig identity is now consistently "the header up to the first whitespace"
			(metawrap2.io.seqio.contig_id) everywhere contigs are compared between files.
			Current metaBAT2 (2.18) appends " total_depth=.. sample_depths=.." to every
			contig header; MetaWrap2 keyed on the *whole* header in some places and on the
			first token in others, so metaBAT2 bins matched nothing:
			- bin_refinement measured 0% overlap between a metaBAT2 bin and the same bin
			  from another binner (the threshold is 80%), so consolidation silently never
			  combined metaBAT2 with MaxBin2/CONCOCT - the module appeared to work and
			  quietly produced a worse bin set
			- quant_bins aborted outright with "None of the contigs/scaffolds in the -a
			  metagenomic assembly file were present in the bin files"
			metaBAT2's bins are also normalised at the source now, so MetaWrap2's own
			outputs carry clean headers
	Fixed - data loss and wrong results
		read_qc with --skip-trimming --skip-bmtagger moved the user's *raw input* FASTQ into
			the output directory (os.replace on a path outside the output dir). It now copies
			when the source is not an intermediate it created
		read_qc left the decompressed copies of gzipped inputs in the output directory
			forever - several GB per sample of pure duplication. They are now removed once
			the final reads are written
		binning split CONCOCT clusters by truncating each contig name at the *first* ".",
			so any assembler whose names contain a dot - metaSPAdes emits
			"NODE_1_length_5000_cov_3.5" - had unrelated contigs collapsed onto one key and
			scattered into the wrong bins. Names are now used verbatim. Splitting is also
			idempotent now (it appended, so a re-run duplicated every sequence)
		metaBAT2's --unbinned output (bin.unbinned.fa) sat in the bin directory where
			bin_refinement and CheckM scored it as if it were a genome; it is moved aside
		rm_short_contigs parsed the contig length out of a SPAdes-style header and stopped at
			the first short contig; it now measures the sequence, so it works on any
			assembler and does not depend on the file being sorted longest-first
		refinement.dereplicate raised KeyError and aborted the whole run if a bin file had no
			row in the .stats file; such bins now lose contested contigs and a warning is printed
	Fixed - --dry-run
		--dry-run only ever printed the first one or two commands of a module: the
			orchestration then tried to consume outputs that a dry run never produced and
			died with a raw traceback, or with a misleading "Something went wrong with X".
			Every module now prints its complete pipeline (dry-run-aware filesystem helpers
			in modules/_common.py), writes no result data, and still records the plan in
			run_commands.txt. Covered by a test per module
	Fixed - log noise
		run.stdout/run.stderr are now filtered for runaway repetition and progress-bar
			redraws. Measured on a 3-sample run, INITIAL_BINNING/run.stderr was 1.8 MB, of
			which 23,770 of 24,770 lines were two metaBAT2 warnings repeated ~11,900 times
			each; filtering takes it to 30 KB (98.5% smaller). Repeats are counted, not
			discarded: the first 20 are kept verbatim and a summary line reports how many
			more there were. A command whose stdout *is* its result is never filtered, the
			error message in a ToolError still shows the tool's real last output, and
			--verbose-logs restores the raw behaviour
		a failed per-bin SPAdes reassembly now reports the ERROR lines from that bin's
			spades.log instead of guessing "It is possible there were not enough reads",
			and the all-bins-failed message lists the likely shared causes
	Fixed - scalability and robustness
		"kraken2 --subsample" materialised every read of both files in memory before
			sampling, so it died on exactly the large libraries it exists to shrink; it now
			uses reservoir sampling, bounded by the subsample size
		the BLAST database no longer has to be named "nt" with 2-digit volume numbering:
			BLASTDB_NAME selects the database, and presence is detected across single-volume,
			2-digit, 3-digit, and alias (.nal) layouts (BLAST v5 uses 3 digits)
		conda_env_exists guessed at a couple of hard-coded prefixes; it now asks conda/mamba
			for the real environment directories
		"metawrap2 install-env" located envs/ by a fixed relative path that only works in a
			source checkout; it now searches upward and honours $METAWRAP2_ENVS_DIR
		fix_megahit_contig_naming read len=/multi= by position, which breaks across MEGAHIT
			releases; it now reads them by name and falls back to the measured length
		a bin_refinement run whose consolidation yields no bins now explains that nothing
			met the -c/-x thresholds and points at the .stats files, instead of failing
			inside CheckM with "No bins found. Check the extension (-x) used to identify bins"
		every module now makes its path arguments absolute up front (modules._common.
			absolutize_paths). Tools that must run with cwd=<output dir> because they write
			into the working directory - taxator-tk's binner, the plotting helpers - were
			being handed paths relative to the *original* directory, producing
			"cd out && ... out/file" (i.e. out/out/file). So classify_bins and the final
			bin_refinement plot both failed for anyone who passed a relative -o, which is
			what the tutorial itself does
		binner and taxknife now get TAXATORTK_TAXONOMY_NCBI, which only taxator had. binner
			aborted with "Specify the folder containing the NCBI taxonomy dump files as
			TAXATORTK_TAXONOMY_NCBI environment variable" - after the slow megablast step
			had already completed
		kraken2 looked for names.dmp/nodes.dmp only under <db>/taxonomy/, which is the
			kraken2-build layout; every *prebuilt* database keeps them at the top level, so
			the module crashed with a bare FileNotFoundError after the slow classification
			had already finished. It now searches both and falls back to TAXDUMP
		"superkingdom" is no longer assumed: NCBI renamed that rank to "domain" and a
			current taxdump has zero superkingdom nodes, so blobology's superkingdom
			blobplot said "Not annotated" for every contig - losing the
			Bacteria/Archaea/Eukaryota split the plot exists for - and prune_blast_hits
			discarded domain-ranked hits. Both ranks are now accepted as synonyms
		metaBAT2's non-genome outputs (bin.lowDepth.fa, bin.tooShort.fa, bin.unbinned.fa
			and its two .txt summaries) are moved out of the bin directory into
			metabat2_extra/ so CheckM and bin_refinement do not score them as genomes
		gc_cov_annotate shells out to samtools, which is in the blobology conda env and not
			on the host PATH where the helper now runs; the caller passes its absolute path
		seaborn's clustermap needs scipy, which was not declared - the quant_bins heatmap
			failed at the very last step of the module
		annotate_bins iterated every file in the bin folder, including .stats/.contigs
			sidecars; it now only annotates fasta files, and says so if there are none
		sam_to_fastq wrote a ">" header into a 4-line FASTQ record, making the file neither
			valid FASTA nor valid FASTQ; it now writes "@" (verified to be equivalent for
			MEGAHIT, the only in-pipeline consumer)
	New - opt-in modern tools, now actually wired up
		"bin_refinement --checkm2" scores bins with CheckM2 instead of CheckM1: fast, no
			pplacer, none of CheckM1's ~40GB-per-thread memory wall. Produces the same
			.stats contract, so every later step works unchanged
		"classify_bins --gtdbtk" classifies against GTDB with GTDB-Tk in place of the
			MEGABLAST -> taxator-tk -> binner -> taxknife chain, writing the same
			bin_taxonomy.tab
		"annotate_bins --bakta" annotates with Bakta instead of the unmaintained PROKKA,
			with the same output layout (--bakta-db or BAKTA_DB)
		these three environments shipped in 2.0.0 with no flags to select them, and the docs
			described flags that did not exist
	New - installation and usability
		"metawrap2 install-db" downloads, unpacks, indexes, and *configures* every database,
			including the "checkm data setRoot" step that is easy to forget and otherwise
			surfaces much later as a confusing bin_refinement failure. "--list" shows sizes
			and which module needs what; "--small" installs capped-but-real builds for
			testing; already-installed databases are skipped, so it is safe to re-run
		"install-env -t N" and "install-db -t N" do the work N at a time (env solves and
			database downloads are independent and network-bound, so this is close to a
			linear speedup). Per-env solver output goes to its own log file instead of
			interleaving on screen
		"-t/--threads" now defaults to [settings] threads from metawrap2.toml instead of a
			silent 1 - which on a metagenome was the difference between hours and days - and
			accepts "-t all". Out-of-range values are clamped with a warning
		a module whose conda env is missing now fails immediately with the exact
			"metawrap2 install-env <module>" command to fix it, instead of a mamba error
			about a missing prefix part-way into a long run
		progress bars (tqdm) on the long loops that used to look like a hang: read
			recruitment, per-bin reassembly and annotation, CheckM over bin sets,
			per-sample quantification and mapping, CONCOCT bin splitting, subsampling.
			They go to stderr and switch off when stderr is not a terminal, so they never
			corrupt a redirected data file or flood run.stderr
		"metawrap2 check" now exits non-zero when a selected module is missing a database it
			cannot run without (it only ever checked conda envs, despite saying otherwise),
			and no longer reports setting-valued keys like BLASTDB_NAME as a missing path
		trim_galore now gets -j (capped at 4, where cutadapt stops scaling) instead of
			running single-threaded regardless of -t
	Testing
		added 50+ regression tests pinning every fix above, and made two environment-
			dependent tests hermetic (one passed only before the conda envs existed)

MetaWrap2 v=2.0.0
	MetaWrap2 is the Python-native successor to metaWRAP. Same algorithms, same external
	tools, same results as metaWRAP 1.x - but the pipeline is now entirely Python (no Bash,
	Perl, or R), with modern packaging and per-module conda environments.
	General
		command is now "metawrap2" (Python package "metawrap2"); modules are unchanged
		every module is now a Python module: `metawrap2 <module>` or `python -m metawrap2.modules.<module>`
		each module runs in its own conda environment "metawrap2-<module>" holding only that
			module's tools, so environments resolve quickly and independently
		commands each module runs are visible, editable templates at the top of each module file;
			any surfaced constant can also be overridden via ~/.metawrap2/config.toml
		configuration moved from the sourced config-metawrap file to metawrap2.toml
		removed all Bash, Perl, and R; blobology and Binning_refiner steps now run in Python
		"metawrap2 check" verifies tools, conda envs, and databases before a run;
			"metawrap2 install-env" creates (and verifies) a module's conda environment;
			"metawrap2 test" probes each module env + key software, runs unit tests, and
			prints an installed/missing/broken status map
		each run writes provenance into its output dir: run_config.json (full config +
			defaults + timing + tool versions used), run_commands.txt (exact commands,
			re-runnable), provenance.txt (full lineage, propagated from any MetaWrap2
			inputs used), and run.stdout/run.stderr (full tool output, also shown live)
		all external commands run through a single command handler that wraps them in the
			module's conda env, streams output to screen and to run.stdout/run.stderr, records
			them for provenance, and cleans up child processes on Ctrl-C
		conda environments are created and driven with mamba (faster); install-env
			bootstraps mamba if needed. Set use_conda_envs=false to run tools off PATH
		global options: "--dry-run" (record/print commands without running), "--force"
			(overwrite an existing output dir; otherwise MetaWrap2 refuses to clobber a run),
			and "--resume" (reuse an output dir and skip stages that already finished, tracked
			under <outdir>/.metawrap2/steps)
		"metawrap2 config init|show" scaffolds and inspects metawrap2.toml;
			"metawrap2 completion bash|zsh" prints a shell-completion script
		"metawrap2 test" now reports the version of each working tool (--verbose lists all)
		warns when input/output paths contain spaces or shell metacharacters
		project tooling: CI tests across Python 3.8-3.12 with coverage, a pre-commit config,
			CONTRIBUTING guide, issue/PR templates, and an mkdocs documentation site
		flowchart figures are regenerated in Python from their draw.io sources
			(flowcharts/make_flowcharts.py); no Perl/R/draw.io needed
		dropped the deprecated kraken1 module (use kraken2) and metaBAT1 binner (use metaBAT2)
	Coming from metaWRAP 1.x
		replace "metaWRAP" with "metawrap2" in your commands; module names and outputs are the same
		see README "Coming from metaWRAP 1.x" for the full flag/output mapping

metaWRAP v=1.5 (superseded by MetaWrap2 2.0.0)
	General
		Python scripts now run under Python 3 (Python 2 reached end of life)
		sequence inputs can now be gzip- or bzip2-compressed (.gz/.bz2)
		added "check" command to verify tools and databases are available before a run
		dependencies loosened from old fixed versions and grouped by module under envs/
		clearer, tool-specific error messages when an external program fails
		reset the example database paths in config-metawrap to generic placeholders
	Read_QC
		gzipped read files (.gz) are now accepted as input
	Bin_refinement
		use a portable method to measure bin file sizes (works on macOS/BSD)
		bin consolidation is now deterministic regardless of input ordering
	Blobology
		bowtie2-build now uses the requested number of threads
		replaced the Perl and R helper scripts with Python; the contig annotation
			table and blobplot images are now generated with Python (no Perl/R needed)
	Reassemble_bins
		reassembly now uses one thread per bin as documented, reducing oversubscription

